# SwiftTerm vendor pin — read by app/Scripts/verify-vendor.sh
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# vendor/ is gitignored (.gitignore:13), vendor/SwiftTerm is not itself a git repo,
# app/Package.swift:20 is an unpinned `.package(path:)`, and Package.resolved is
# ignored too (.gitignore:6). So before this pin existed, NOTHING in the repository
# recorded which upstream revision was vendored or that a local patch had been
# applied to it. The failure was asymmetric and that is the whole problem:
#
#   * a MISSING vendor/ fails loudly  — SwiftPM cannot resolve the path dependency
#   * a RE-VENDORED, UNPATCHED tree fails SILENTLY — it compiles and runs fine,
#     because the patch only removes a build-time resource
#
# So following app/README.md's recreation steps faithfully but forgetting a patch
# produced a green build with a different dependency than the one that was tested.
# verify-vendor.sh turns that silent case into a loud one.
#
# As of patch 0002 (Buffer.swift, the #494 reflow fix) the stakes are higher: a tree
# missing 0002 does not merely build differently, it silently CORRUPTS SCROLLBACK when a
# window is narrowed. That is why the Buffer.swift check below is a hard failure.
#
# VERIFIED PROVENANCE (2026-07-27, amended 2026-07-29)
# A full recursive diff of the vendored tree against the upstream v1.15.0 tarball
# reported exactly ONE differing entry: Package.swift. Everything else — all 190+
# source files including Buffer.swift and Terminal.swift — was byte-identical to
# upstream.
#
# As of 2026-07-29 there are TWO differing entries: Package.swift and
# Buffer.swift. The vendored tree is upstream v1.15.0 + two local patches.
#
# Regenerate the hashes below with:
#   shasum -a 256 vendor/SwiftTerm/Package.swift
#   shasum -a 256 vendor/SwiftTerm/Sources/SwiftTerm/Buffer.swift
#   shasum -a 256 vendor/SwiftTerm/Sources/SwiftTerm/Terminal.swift

upstream_repo   = https://github.com/migueldeicaza/SwiftTerm
upstream_tag    = v1.15.0
upstream_commit = dd2fb8ac5b861e7bf617c872895e338f38165648

# Package.swift AFTER applying 0001-exclude-metal-shader-from-target.patch.
# This is the hash verify-vendor.sh requires.
patched_package_swift  = 90e0a07aab0bd278fcb6d10902158e1f3314063de603c7df627a87b58fc4fab6

# Package.swift as shipped upstream at v1.15.0. Matching THIS hash instead means the
# tree was re-vendored and the patch was never applied — the silent-failure case, and
# the specific reason this file exists. verify-vendor.sh reports it by name.
upstream_package_swift = a1dfb1317409a108ad5c9d8db83e5d058bdbbd700ecbf8892f27a498ac200575

# Buffer.swift AFTER applying ALL THREE of 0002-index-iswrapped-buffer-absolute.patch,
# 0003-trim-lines-on-narrowing-for-all-buffers.patch and
# 0004-gate-resize-post-condition-behind-debug.patch. One hash covers all three: they are
# independent hunks of the same file (0002 near line 1170, 0003 near line 520, 0004 near
# line 544), and a single hash is deliberately unforgiving — a tree carrying only some of
# them matches neither this nor the upstream hash and verify-vendor.sh calls it "unknown"
# (exit 3). This is the hash verify-vendor.sh requires.
#
# That combined hash is also why 0004 needs no check of its own even though, unlike 0002
# and 0003, its absence corrupts nothing. A tree missing it is merely slower and carries a
# release-build `abort()`; it still cannot pass, because it cannot match this hash. The
# existing "half-patched is not a state this project supports" rule covers it for free.
#
# This file used to be pinned as a version TRIPWIRE with the note "it is unmodified
# upstream and must stay that way while option A is unchosen". That is no longer true,
# and the sentence was removed rather than softened: Buffer.swift now carries REQUIRED
# patches, so this is a patch check exactly like Package.swift above, not a tripwire.
#
# What 0002 fixes — the reflow defect of record: upstream lines 1171 and 1211 set
# `_lines[_y].isWrapped = true` using a SCREEN-relative index, while the adjacent content
# writes at 1176 and 1224 correctly use the BUFFER-absolute `_lines[_y + _yBase]` (`_y` is
# screen-relative per Buffer.swift:22, 40-43). With non-empty scrollback the wrapped-line
# flag therefore landed `_yBase` rows off target and reflow mangled history. See
# .afk/plans/emulator-foundation-probe-and-vendor-integrity.md §2 and SwiftTerm issue #494.
#
# What 0003 fixes — the ALT-buffer resize defect: upstream keeps the "trim each line to
# the new width" loop inside `if isReflowEnabled`, and `isReflowEnabled` is just
# `hasScrollback` (Buffer.swift:419), which is false for the alt buffer (built
# `scrollback: nil`, Terminal.swift:694). Narrowing therefore shrank `cols` while every
# BufferLine kept its old width, and the next WIDENING copied those stale cells back into
# the visible grid (BufferLine.swift:197). Under tmux — which lives in the alt buffer and
# repaints only deltas — that is content bleeding across pane and window boundaries.
# See .afk/research/tmux-bleed-altbuffer-resize-2026-07-29.md.
#
# What 0004 fixes — a debug assertion that ships. Upstream wraps this file's other five
# debug blocks in `#if DEBUG` (:47, :62, :81, :98, :116) but leaves the `// DEBUG:
# Post-condition` loop after the resize trim ungated. Verified against the pristine
# v1.15.0 file, where it sits at :533-546 with no surrounding conditional — so this is an
# upstream leftover, not something 0002/0003 introduced. It walks the viewport PLUS THE
# ENTIRE SCROLLBACK on every resize (the third such walk in the function, after reflow and
# after 0003's trim) and calls `abort()` on mismatch. Every size change reaches it:
# processSizeChange (live window drag — once per drag frame — plus ⌘B and full screen) and
# resetFont (⌘+/⌘-/⌘0, ⌘R). At Umber's default 1000-line scrollback that is 1000 iterations
# per buffer per resize event, and a hard crash path raised from inside a window drag in a
# terminal whose whole job is hosting long-running agent sessions.
# See .afk/research/performance-audit-2026-07-31.md §2.
#
# 0002 and 0003 are verified behaviourally, and each gate was validated by falsification:
# app/Scripts/check-reflow.sh FAILS 6 of 8 cases without 0002, and
# app/Scripts/check-altbuffer-resize.sh FAILS 2 of 4 without 0003 (cases 1 and 2, while
# its control and its render-range case keep passing). If you re-vendor, run both.
#
# 0004 gets NO behavioural gate, deliberately, and this is the honest reason: it changes no
# release behaviour that any check could observe. Its whole effect is deleting work and
# deleting a crash path, so a passing gate would prove only that the compiler honours
# `#if DEBUG`. What guards it instead is this pin's hash plus the fact that both remaining
# Buffer.swift gates still pass with it applied — i.e. that removing the assertion did not
# remove anything the assertion was catching. Both were re-run when 0004 landed.
patched_buffer_swift  = 05d64bab59de389334e0557b7cbf776551d0cf3d9bf9ab4d59df09736d31c8d1

# Buffer.swift as shipped upstream at v1.15.0. Matching THIS hash means the tree was
# re-vendored and NEITHER 0002 nor 0003 was applied — the silent-failure case, and now the
# WORSE of the two files: patch 0001 only drops a build-time resource, but a Buffer.swift
# missing its patches compiles, runs, and silently corrupts the buffer — 0002's absence
# mangles scrollback on every window narrowing, 0003's bleeds stale cells across tmux panes
# on every widening. verify-vendor.sh reports it by name and FAILS (it only warned while
# Buffer.swift was unmodified upstream).
upstream_buffer_swift = 7b65f2b9ce21c95b124848b76856e727fd8a407cb7354a1dc19ab82f4d2c16bd

# Terminal.swift is still a pure version tripwire — unmodified upstream, no patch. The
# reflow read-back API check-reflow.sh depends on lives here
# (getBufferAsData, Terminal.swift:5947), so a change means re-checking that gate.
terminal_swift = 7d29fe260ff9561ac827e3e145838005c1ad5306f5ca0d087fec2b813520ede9
